Steps before running

1. Check your WiFi adapter supports monitor mode

 • You need an adapter with a monitor-mode-capable chipset (e.g., Alfa AWUS036ACH, TP-Link TL-WN722N v1).
 • Most built-in laptop cards have limited support.

2. Use Linux with root access

 • Kali Linux, Parrot OS, or Ubuntu work best.
 • You must be able to run commands with sudo.

3. Install the dependencies


sudo apt update

sudo apt install iw aircrack-ng

pip3 install scapy


 • iw — used by the channel hopper
 • aircrack-ng — provides airmon-ng for monitor mode setup
 • scapy — the packet capture/parsing library

4. Kill processes that interfere with the WiFi card


sudo airmon-ng check kill


⚠️ This disconnects you from the internet (kills NetworkManager) — that's normal.

5. Put the adapter into monitor mode


sudo airmon-ng start wlan0


 • This creates a new interface, usually named wlan0mon.
 • Verify it exists with: iw dev (look for Interface wlan0mon and type monitor).

6. Confirm the interface name

 • Note the monitor interface name (e.g., wlan0mon) — you'll pass it with -i when running.

7. (Optional) If you built the executable

 • Build it on the same OS you plan to run it on: python3 build_exe.py
 • The output lands in dist/wifi_sniffer (Linux) or dist/wifi_sniffer.exe (Windows).

8. (Optional) Run the self-test first


python3 wifi_sniffer_app.py --self-test


 • Confirms the crypto core works before you go live.

Once these are done, you're ready to run:


sudo python3 wifi_sniffer_app.py -i wlan0mon


